# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
#
# THE definition of the frozen-artifact size ceilings. Two readers, one file:
# `scripts/build_binary.sh` sources it (`.` -- exact, no parsing), and
# `tests/conformance/test_packaged_binary.py` parses these `KEY=VALUE` lines.
# The two numbers used to be copied into both, and they drifted into rejecting a
# CORRECT build (see below) with nothing pointing at the other copy. Editing
# this file changes both readers, which is the only property that matters here.
#
# Format is deliberately dumb so `sh` can source it: `KEY=VALUE`, no quotes, no
# spaces around `=`, `#` comments only. Nothing else may live in this file.
#
# WHAT THE CEILING IS FOR: catching a build made from a DIRTY interpreter.
# PyInstaller bundles whatever its hooks can see, so an interpreter that happens
# to carry numpy/Pillow/pywin32 inflates the artifact -- measured 34349423 B
# against 10237542 B clean at the time -- and the unpack cost lands on every
# `tan` invocation. The extension's 3 s `--version` probe does NOT catch it: the
# dirty 34349423 B build answered in ~1.00 s and passed every timing assertion.
# Size is the only signal that separates the two.
#
# WHY TWO NUMBERS: one number cannot describe both libcs. musl links libc
# statically, so a musl asset is legitimately ~1.0 MB (x86_64) to ~1.4 MB
# (aarch64) larger than its glibc twin. Measured, clean venvs, PyInstaller
# 6.21.0, `--onefile`:
#
#   tan-x86_64-pc-windows-msvc.exe   13517737 B   native Windows 11 x86_64
#   tan-aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu    13840528 B   python:3.12-slim-bullseye, arm64
#   tan-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu     14042352 B   python:3.12-slim-bullseye
#   tan-x86_64-unknown-linux-musl    14998816 B   python:3.12-alpine
#   tan-aarch64-unknown-linux-musl   15277408 B   python:3.12-alpine, arm64
#
# The single 15000000 ceiling REJECTED that last one -- a build that then passed
# every proof in a Python-free arm64 alpine -- while the x86_64 musl build
# cleared it by 1184 bytes purely by luck.
#
# HOW THESE TWO NUMBERS WERE PICKED: each sits >=1.2 MB above the largest
# measured clean build of its class, so ordinary growth (a new dependency, a
# CPython bump) does not trip it, and each stays under 52% of the 34349423 B
# dirty measurement, so a dirty build is still unambiguously over. If a build
# lands between its ceiling and ~34 MB, treat it as a real question about what
# got bundled -- not as a number to nudge.
#
# HOW STRONG THE DIRTY SIGNAL ACTUALLY IS: weaker than "any stray package".
# Re-measured 2026-07-30 with PyInstaller 6.21.0: a build venv carrying numpy
# 2.5.1 produced 13516327 B, against 13517584 B from the same venv without it --
# no inflation at all, because PyInstaller bundles what the import graph reaches
# and `tan` never imports numpy. The 34349423 B measurement came from an
# interpreter carrying pywin32/Pillow-class packages, whose HOOKS collect data
# files and binaries regardless. So this ceiling catches the hook-heavy case,
# not every dirty venv -- which is the other reason `build_binary.sh` now
# recommends `pip install -e .` (the declared set) rather than trusting size
# alone to notice a richer environment.
#
# UNMEASURED: macOS. No macOS host was available, so neither
# `tan-x86_64-apple-darwin` nor `tan-aarch64-apple-darwin` has a number here;
# both fall under the DEFAULT ceiling. PyInstaller's macOS bootloader is
# universal2 and may carry more than the Linux one. If the first macOS build
# lands between DEFAULT and ~34 MB, measure it and give macOS its own line here
# rather than raising DEFAULT for every platform.
TAN_MAX_ARTIFACT_BYTES_DEFAULT=16500000
TAN_MAX_ARTIFACT_BYTES_MUSL=18000000
